GRX regression probes
=====================

Six self-contained programs, no application code, each exercising one GRX
primitive.  They exist because porting this program to Windows turned up two
GRX bugs that Linux hides completely - fresh heap pages are zero there, and
long is 64 bit - so the way to find them was to run the same primitive on
both platforms and compare bytes.

Run them on both and diff the output:

    ./run-tests.sh              # host
    ./run-tests.sh --windows    # mingw + wine

What each one asserts
---------------------

sttzero.c           sttzero() must clear all of a GrContext.  It cleared 52
                    of 104 bytes on Win64, leaving gc_xoffset/gc_yoffset as
                    garbage that was then added to every draw coordinate.
                    Pass: "bytes still non-zero after sttzero: 0".

alloc-colour.c      GrAllocColor() must return the same packed value on
                    every platform.  Pass: all six lines identical to Linux.

colour-readback.c   Draw a box, then read the bytes back out of the surface.
                    Catches colours that allocate correctly but do not get
                    written.  Pass: bytes match the colour, on both.

fill-runs-8bpp.c    Fill widths 1..8 in an 8bpp context and count the bytes
                    actually written.  w=8 wrote 4 bytes on Win64 - that is
                    what made gen-bitmap's raster ragged.
                    Pass: every width fills exactly w bytes.

fill-runs-24bpp.c   Same idea at 24bpp, for equal-byte colours (which take
                    driver24.h's shortcut) and unequal ones (general path).
                    A 4 pixel run of 0xc0c0c0 wrote 9 of 12 bytes.
                    Pass: every run fully filled.  Note the "<-- ZEROS" tag
                    on the 0080c0 rows is expected: that colour legitimately
                    has a zero component.

clear-overrun.c     Supply our own frame buffer with a guard region behind
                    it, clear the context, and check the guard.  This is the
                    one that first caught the uninitialised context.
                    Pass: "clean" at every geometry, no crash.

Both bugs traced back to one line in src/include/libgrx.h, which keyed its
integer types on architecture names and claimed long was 64 bit on mingw.
See grx-patches/ for the fixes; patch 02 is the root cause, 04 and 05 are
the two symptoms that were fixed first.


Not run by run-tests.sh
=======================

zdr-roundtrip.py    Pushes all 256 of uvknew's internal ZDR values through
                    the whole chain - uvknew's encode arithmetic, debufr's
                    _DIF table imported from bufr2wrk.py, and the formula
                    read back out of showdata.c - and reports what survives.
                    Needs no BUFR file and no radar; it is arithmetic.
                    Run it after touching any of those three.
                    Today: 126 of 255 values survive, faithful over
                    -4.6 .. +8.1 dB.  The rest cannot survive - see below.

scroll-cost.c       A benchmark, not a pass/fail check, so it is left out of
                    the TESTS list.  Times the three parts of one draw_map()
                    call - the map into RAM, the blit to the screen, and the
                    legend - against whichever driver is named on the command
                    line.  Run it as "scroll-cost" and again as "scroll-cost
                    memory" to see why cursor key scrolling crawls under X11
                    and not on Windows: a scroll step costs about 40 ms
                    through the X server and about 1 ms against a linear
                    frame buffer, which is what the Win32 GDI driver is.
                    Build it by hand, see the header comment.


bufr/, bufr-tyumen/, mkdata.py, data/
=====================================

A small archive to render, so a build can be checked on a platform nobody
here can boot.  The macOS workflow writes a paths file with "MAP tests/data",
renders one frame per product family, and keeps the PNGs as artifacts - which
is how a macOS build gets looked at without owning a Mac.

bufr/       27 BUFR messages from Krasnodar (bulletin code RAKD, WMO 39408)
            as they arrive, plus the DEBUFR.CFg that bufr2wrk.py needs for
            the local time offset and the station table.

bufr-tyumen/
            20 messages from Tyumen (RATN, WMO 39692) for one observation,
            2026-08-10 18:50: reflectivity levels 1-10 and differential
            reflectivity levels 1-10, with that feed's own DEBUFR.CFg.  Ten
            levels of one family is what a vertical section needs, and ZDR
            is the scale that wraps, so this is the frame the ZDR GeoTIFF
            checks and cross-section-test.py both cut through.  The rasters
            it decodes to are byte-identical to the operational archive's.

mkdata.py   Rebuilds data/ from them through ../bufr2wrk.py.  One BUFR
            message carries one product, so a frame is the set of messages
            sharing a station and an observation time; mkdata.py groups them
            and writes the zips.  Pure standard library, as bufr2wrk.py is,
            so nothing has to be installed to regenerate the fixture:

                python3 tests/mkdata.py            rebuild
                python3 tests/mkdata.py --list     show what the BUFR holds

data/       The result: portN/YYMMDDHH.MMm zips of .wrk rasters plus
            header.wrk.  Three frames, all real observations -

                22012322.00m  25 products: dbz 0-11, ZDR 2-10, storm,
                              cloud top, phenomena, 1 h rainfall
                25012510.50m   2 products: radial velocity 1 and 2
                25061106.10m   1 product:  ZDR 1

            and port97 -

                26081018.50m  20 products: dbz 1-10, ZDR 1-10

            The port number is only a directory name here.  The viewer takes
            a port's position from header.wrk - files.c:477 reads bytes 47..52
            as degrees, minutes and seconds - so the station lands in the
            right place whichever portN it is filed under.

Two things to know before using it.

Name the frame you want.  With no arguments the tools open the newest frame,
which in this fixture is the one carrying a single ZDR raster; ask for
reflectivity there and read_files() reports "no product in this file" and
draws an empty map.  Pass time<HH>:<MM> to pick the frame and map<N> / zdr<N>
/ vel<N> / sum<N> / top / phenom to pick the product.  A test that takes the
defaults measures an empty map and still passes on file size alone.

Keep MAP relative.  mapdir is char[80] in files.c, so a long absolute path is
silently truncated and the archive simply appears to be missing.

Locally:

    printf 'WRK RAB\nMAP tests/data\nCFG config\nGRF config\n' > paths
    ./gen-bitmap time22:00 zdr2
    QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen IMAGEQT_SHOT=shot.png ./imageqt time22:00 map1

geotiff-test.sh / geotiff-check.py
    Renders tests/data to GeoTIFF, one file per product family, and reads each
    one back: size, colour map, georeferencing, the per product no-data byte
    and the pixels themselves.  Also checks that the seven come out different
    and that a product the frame does not carry is refused rather than quietly
    filled in with another.  Run it from anywhere:

        ./tests/geotiff-test.sh
        BIN=dist/gen-bitmap ./tests/geotiff-test.sh

    geotiff-check.py parses the TIFF directly - standard library only, so no
    GDAL is needed on a CI runner.  It writes paths-test rather than paths, to
    leave the tracked one pointing at the real archive.
